WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.950
Welcome to the Federal
Modernization Workbook's

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.638
developer workbook,
Building Azure Security.

3
00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:09.313
This is Chapter 8: policy,
drift and evidence.

4
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:13.718
An exemption without an expiry is a
decision nobody has to make again,

5
00:00:13.718 --> 00:00:18.587
and a finding without last week's run
beside it is a number nobody can explain.

6
00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:22.900
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's
policy samples for Resource Graph,

7
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.154
see what they leave to you,
read the kit's policy check,

8
00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:31.224
its comparison of two runs, its metrics and
its evidence records, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:34.240
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

10
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.510
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

11
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.373
Why this chapter?

12
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:47.200
Because policy holds everything
the earlier chapters checked,

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.511
and evidence is what an assessor reads.

14
00:00:49.511 --> 00:00:54.146
By the end you can check the security
initiative and its exemptions every week,

15
00:00:54.146 --> 00:00:56.856
say what changed since
last week in every area,

16
00:00:56.856 --> 00:01:00.136
report the guide's metrics
with the numbers behind them,

17
00:01:00.136 --> 00:01:04.630
and hand the security officer an evidence
folder whose every file is hashed,

18
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.199
whose records form a chain,

19
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:10.335
and whose records name the controls
and indicators each run supports.

20
00:01:10.335 --> 00:01:14.987
It's for policy owners and security
officers, for the platform owners who run the

21
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:20.070
weekly job, and for the developers who keep
the comparison and the records honest.

22
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.695
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

23
00:01:25.695 --> 00:01:29.727
Learn's Resource Graph samples
for Azure Policy read assignments,

24
00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:32.732
exemptions and compliance
states from one table.

25
00:01:32.732 --> 00:01:36.813
Its compliance-by-assignment query
gives each assignment's state,

26
00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:40.671
percentage and counts, and the
kit uses it exactly as published.

27
00:01:40.671 --> 00:01:45.045
Another sample lists the exemptions
due to expire within ninety days.

28
00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:49.858
Learn's exemption structure says an
exemption is either mitigated or a waiver,

29
00:01:49.858 --> 00:01:54.745
can carry an expiry, and once expired is
kept for the record but no longer honored.

30
00:01:54.745 --> 00:01:58.988
And DoNotEnforce evaluates without
enforcing, which is where the guide's

31
00:01:58.988 --> 00:02:03.720
Step 9 starts before switching to
enforcement after the compliance review.

32
00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.695
What do Learn and the guide leave to you?

33
00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:14.845
The ninety-day list misses an exemption
with no expiry, and one already expired.

34
00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:18.406
"Switch to enforcement after
the review" is a sentence,

35
00:02:18.406 --> 00:02:21.320
so an assignment can
sit in audit for months.

36
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.282
So is "no excluded
scopes for convenience".

37
00:02:24.282 --> 00:02:28.658
One month's compliance export alone
can't show whether anything got better.

38
00:02:28.658 --> 00:02:31.870
The guide's metrics each need
numbers from a different step.

39
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.190
And a folder of exports doesn't
show that nothing changed

40
00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.157
after it was filed, or which
control a file supports.

41
00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:42.160
The kit answers each, and
a test pins each answer.

42
00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.436
The policy check reads the assignments,

43
00:02:48.436 --> 00:02:52.647
the exemptions and Learn's compliance
query, and reports six rules.

44
00:02:52.647 --> 00:02:56.247
The security initiative must be
assigned at the intermediate root.

45
00:02:56.247 --> 00:02:58.681
It's a warning if it's
still in DoNotEnforce

46
00:02:58.681 --> 00:03:01.185
after the enforce-by
date the settings give.

47
00:03:01.185 --> 00:03:06.595
Every exemption needs an expiry; one past
its expiry is a finding, because Learn

48
00:03:06.595 --> 00:03:11.673
says it's kept but no longer honored;
one due within thirty days is a warning.

49
00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:15.747
And an assignment that excludes
scopes with notScopes is a finding.

50
00:03:15.747 --> 00:03:20.910
Most of these are the program's design
guidance; the expiry rule is Learn's.

51
00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.823
Three more tools.

52
00:03:25.823 --> 00:03:28.698
The comparison takes
two runs and, per area,

53
00:03:28.698 --> 00:03:33.710
compares findings and warnings by
fingerprint, the rule and the resource.

54
00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:39.460
Only in the later run is new; only in the
earlier is fixed; in both, still open.

55
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.125
The metrics tool computes eight of the
guide's twelve metrics from one run,

56
00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:47.795
with the numbers behind each;
private PaaS reports a count,

57
00:03:47.795 --> 00:03:50.931
because Resource Graph's
list has no denominator;

58
00:03:50.931 --> 00:03:55.060
and three come from exercise records
and the workspace, and say so.

59
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.544
And the evidence tool records any run:
every file's hash, the hash of the

60
00:03:59.544 --> 00:04:04.185
record before it, and the controls
and indicators of the steps it covers.

61
00:04:04.185 --> 00:04:07.290
A fixture run never counts toward a stage.

62
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.090
Who's involved?

63
00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.484
The policy owner owns the
initiative and its exemptions,

64
00:04:15.484 --> 00:04:19.890
and reads the drift report at the weekly
review with the security officer.

65
00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:24.115
The platform owner runs the weekly
job and answers for new rows.

66
00:04:24.115 --> 00:04:27.458
And the security officer
keeps the evidence folder,

67
00:04:27.458 --> 00:04:29.790
on agency storage, and runs its check.

68
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.677
Every read is still Reader.

69
00:04:31.677 --> 00:04:36.606
Findings from a Zero Trust assessment
enter the workbook's closure chain,

70
00:04:36.606 --> 00:04:41.130
and the Zero Trust Assessment results
kit files them under its DDoS,

71
00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.231
WAF and public exposure family,

72
00:04:43.231 --> 00:04:48.440
whose ready-for-closure evidence is
this kit's register, DDoS and WAF runs.

73
00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.665
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7
with Pester, in the kit's folder.

74
00:04:56.665 --> 00:05:01.501
Between the two weeks, Contoso's platform
team switched the WAF to prevention,

75
00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:06.563
moved the firewall to deny, routed a subnet
to the firewall, added a missing header,

76
00:05:06.563 --> 00:05:11.324
turned on purge protection, enforced
the security initiative and required

77
00:05:11.324 --> 00:05:13.968
authorization for new management groups;

78
00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:16.915
a new public IP appeared
and an orphan went away.

79
00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:21.853
Step 1 runs every area on week
one: forty-eight findings in all.

80
00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:26.643
Step 2 reads the policy findings:
an assignment with excluded scopes,

81
00:05:26.643 --> 00:05:32.190
an exemption expired on October 31, one
with no expiry, one due within thirty days,

82
00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:37.420
and the initiative still in audit
after its November 1 enforce-by date.

83
00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:44.019
Step 3 runs every area on the second week,

84
00:05:44.019 --> 00:05:47.558
with the first week's
register: forty findings now.

85
00:05:47.558 --> 00:05:49.846
Step 4 compares the two weeks.

86
00:05:49.846 --> 00:05:55.113
Thirteen fixed, four new, and all four
new rows come from the two public IPs that

87
00:05:55.113 --> 00:06:00.298
changed: the new one has no log, no owner
and no DDoS protection, and the one that

88
00:06:00.298 --> 00:06:05.895
went away leaves a reminder to remove any
DNS record still pointing at its address.

89
00:06:05.895 --> 00:06:10.951
Among the fixed: the hierarchy setting,
the WAF in detection, the firewall's threat

90
00:06:10.951 --> 00:06:16.510
intelligence and intrusion detection, and
the security initiative's enforcement.

91
00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.235
Step 5 computes the metrics
for the second week.

92
00:06:23.235 --> 00:06:28.364
DDoS coverage is fifty-seven percent,
WAF prevention coverage sixty-seven,

93
00:06:28.364 --> 00:06:34.073
egress inspection sixty, vault posture
thirty-three, and one exemption has expired.

94
00:06:34.073 --> 00:06:38.873
The rows that come from exercise records
say so instead of inventing a number.

95
00:06:38.873 --> 00:06:40.322
Step 6 records the run,

96
00:06:40.322 --> 00:06:44.748
the comparison and the metrics as
evidence, then checks the folder.

97
00:06:44.748 --> 00:06:49.698
The first record covers all nine area
steps and lists sixteen controls.

98
00:06:49.698 --> 00:06:53.173
The comparison and the metrics
each get their own record,

99
00:06:53.173 --> 00:06:56.130
and the chain of three
records is unbroken.

100
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.705
Step 7 asks whether this evidence could
pass the test stage's go or no-go.

101
00:07:04.705 --> 00:07:09.080
Every step fails, with the same
reason: no passing live record.

102
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.280
That's on purpose.

103
00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:12.632
Lab evidence can't promote anything,

104
00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:16.317
so nobody can take a fixture
run to production by mistake.

105
00:07:16.317 --> 00:07:21.300
Step 8 runs this chapter's tests:
ten pass, covering the policy check,

106
00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.830
the run folders, the comparison,
the metrics and the evidence.

107
00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.620
When you're done, delete
the output folder.

108
00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.708
Lab 2 moves to a test management
group with at least one

109
00:07:35.708 --> 00:07:39.120
policy assignment and one
exemption, never production.

110
00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.770
Sign in, write the settings file, and run
every area twice, a few minutes apart.

111
00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.848
With nothing changed between them,

112
00:07:46.848 --> 00:07:49.858
the comparison should show
no new and no fixed rows.

113
00:07:49.858 --> 00:07:54.281
Then compare the policy rows with the
guide's compliance export and Learn's

114
00:07:54.281 --> 00:07:58.633
exemption list: every exemption should
appear, in the state the kit gave it.

115
00:07:58.633 --> 00:08:02.745
Record the second run: its mode is
live, but the stage check still

116
00:08:02.745 --> 00:08:06.857
lacks the preflight and the
scheduled run, which Chapter 9 adds.

117
00:08:06.857 --> 00:08:10.996
Nothing changed in Azure, so backing
out is deleting what the lab wrote.

118
00:08:10.996 --> 00:08:13.350
The authors haven't run this lab.

119
00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:20.698
Each output supports specific
controls from NIST's catalog,

120
00:08:20.698 --> 00:08:23.288
as the guide's requirements
table maps them.

121
00:08:23.288 --> 00:08:26.462
The policy folder supports
baseline configuration,

122
00:08:26.462 --> 00:08:29.637
configuration settings
and continuous monitoring.

123
00:08:29.637 --> 00:08:34.188
The drift report supports configuration
settings and continuous monitoring.

124
00:08:34.188 --> 00:08:38.224
The metrics support continuous
monitoring and audit record review.

125
00:08:38.224 --> 00:08:41.265
And the chained, hashed
records support continuous

126
00:08:41.265 --> 00:08:44.375
monitoring and the protection
of audit information.

127
00:08:44.375 --> 00:08:48.463
The kit's output supports those
controls; the assessor decides.

128
00:08:48.463 --> 00:08:52.879
The key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers:

129
00:08:52.879 --> 00:08:56.938
the agency lines its own evidence up
with them and doesn't report them.

130
00:08:56.938 --> 00:09:00.418
The exercise and after-action
records the guide's Step

131
00:09:00.418 --> 00:09:03.960
10 asks for are people's
work, filed beside the runs.

132
00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.973
What breaks, and what guards against it?

133
00:09:09.973 --> 00:09:14.009
An exemption with no expiry
accepts non-compliance forever,

134
00:09:14.009 --> 00:09:15.947
so the kit flags it every week.

135
00:09:15.947 --> 00:09:19.376
An expired exemption can look
current in an old report,

136
00:09:19.376 --> 00:09:23.109
so the kit names it, because
Learn says it's no longer honored.

137
00:09:23.109 --> 00:09:27.270
A changed query can make rows look
fixed when they only disappeared;

138
00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:32.247
that shows as many fixed rows at once, which
the reviewer reads in the drift report.

139
00:09:32.247 --> 00:09:34.548
Lab evidence can't promote anything,

140
00:09:34.548 --> 00:09:37.847
because fixture records
never count toward a stage.

141
00:09:37.847 --> 00:09:40.898
And evidence edited after
filing fails the check,

142
00:09:40.898 --> 00:09:44.220
because the hashes and
the chain no longer match.

143
00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.320
Here's what done looks like.

144
00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.758
The security initiative is enforcing, or
its enforce-by date is still in the future.

145
00:09:54.758 --> 00:09:59.733
Every exemption has an expiry, and none
is past it without a renewal decision.

146
00:09:59.733 --> 00:10:04.557
The drift report is read at the weekly
review, and every new row has an owner.

147
00:10:04.557 --> 00:10:08.207
The metrics report carries
the numbers behind each value.

148
00:10:08.207 --> 00:10:12.671
And the evidence check passes on a
folder that lives on agency storage.

149
00:10:12.671 --> 00:10:15.699
Next, Chapter 9 is the
runbook that takes all of

150
00:10:15.699 --> 00:10:18.860
this to a test subscription,
then to production.
